From fea14039cf206bd6fef588023b2f44242f705182 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 12:27:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] board: gateway_dk: light the SFP+ port LEDs

The port LEDs stayed dark until Linux took over, which left the panel
blank for every second spent at the U-Boot prompt -- exactly where an
operator is looking for a link.

Read the same three things the recovery kernel's sfp-led driver shows:
MOD_DEF0 for module presence, the MAC's XFI PCS for link, and the MAC's
frame counters for activity. None of them goes near i2c, so a module
holding SDA low cannot darken its own port or its neighbour's. Polling
runs as a cyclic function, which keeps the LEDs live at the prompt and
through the boot delay.

Taking activity from the MAC rather than from U-Boot's own transfers
means the blink follows whatever the wire delivers, including frames
that arrive while nothing is draining them.

The 10G ports are fixed-link, so nothing configures the XFI PCS and it
reports no link however good the fibre is until its MAC is opened.
Open both here. That adds only the opens: FMan and its microcode are
brought up on every boot regardless, by the enumeration behind the
"Net:" banner, and this only moves that a few milliseconds earlier. A
port with an empty cage opens just as readily, since the path touches
neither i2c nor MOD_DEF0 and phylib takes link from the fixed-link node
rather than waiting for autonegotiation, so a missing module cannot
stall the boot.

An opened MAC keeps receiving, into descriptors and buffers in U-Boot's
heap, and eth_fman is not among the drivers dm_remove_devices_active()
removes before the kernel starts. board_quiesce_devices() removes the
MACs opened here, which runs the driver's stop and disables the MAC and
both of its ports. Without it a frame arriving after the handoff lands
in memory the kernel has already reused, until the kernel's own FMan
driver resets the block.

The link bit latches low, so every read is doubled -- the first read
after a drop returns the stale state and the second the live one.

The poll drives its LEDs through the GPIO driver, on the bank that also
carries the EEPROM's write-protect line (GPIO3_00). That driver writes
the whole data register back from its own copy whenever it sets a line,
so program_eeprom's raw register write of WP was undone within 100 ms of
its final lock, leaving the EEPROM writable until reset -- the kernel
included. WP now goes through the driver too.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [product-specific]

Signed-off-by: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
